    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you about how Earth's plates move, what happens at their boundaries and what evidence supports the theory. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Earth's rigid outer shell, the lithosphere, is made of the crust and the uppermost mantle. It is broken into about a dozen large plates and many smaller ones that move a few centimeters per year over the asthenosphere, a hotter, weaker layer of the mantle that flows slowly even though it is solid.",
      "Plates meet at three kinds of boundary. At divergent boundaries, such as the Mid-Atlantic Ridge, plates move apart and rising magma forms new oceanic crust. At convergent boundaries plates collide: denser oceanic lithosphere sinks beneath another plate in subduction, forming deep trenches and volcanic arcs, while two continents collide and crumple into mountain ranges such as the Himalayas. At transform boundaries, such as the San Andreas Fault, plates slide past each other and crust is neither made nor destroyed.",
      "In 1912 Alfred Wegener proposed continental drift, citing the jigsaw fit of the continents, matching rock formations across oceans and fossils such as Mesosaurus and Glossopteris found on continents now far apart. He could not explain what moved the continents, and the idea was widely rejected for decades.",
      "Seafloor studies in the 1950s and 1960s supplied the missing evidence. The ocean floor is youngest at mid-ocean ridges and older with distance from them, and bands of rock record reversals of Earth's magnetic field in matching stripes on both sides of a ridge. Plates are driven mainly by gravity acting on them, especially slab pull as old, cold, dense lithosphere sinks at subduction zones, within the wider flow of heat out of the mantle."
    ],
