    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you how igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic rocks form and how one becomes another. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Rocks are natural solids made of one or more minerals. Geologists sort them into three families by how they form: igneous, sedimentary and metamorphic. The rock cycle describes how Earth's processes turn any one of these into any other over long spans of time.",
      "Igneous rocks form when molten rock cools and crystallizes. Magma that cools slowly deep underground forms intrusive rocks such as granite, whose crystals grow large enough to see. Lava that cools quickly at the surface forms extrusive rocks such as basalt, with tiny crystals, or glassy rocks such as obsidian.",
      "Sedimentary rocks form at the surface. Weathering breaks rock into fragments and dissolved minerals; erosion carries them away; they are deposited in layers, then buried, compacted and cemented into rock. Sandstone and shale form from fragments, while limestone forms mostly from the shells and skeletons of marine organisms. Sedimentary rocks are the ones most likely to contain fossils.",
      "Metamorphic rocks form when existing rock is changed by heat, pressure or hot fluids without melting. Minerals recrystallize and often line up in bands, a texture called foliation. Shale becomes slate and, with more heat and pressure, schist; limestone becomes marble. If the heat is great enough to melt the rock, the result is magma, and the cycle returns to igneous rock."
    ],
