  {
    "title": "Earthquakes and Seismic Waves",
    "category": "earth-space",
    "subject": "Earth Science",
    "topic": "Earthquakes",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you why earthquakes happen, how seismic waves differ, how an epicenter is found and how earthquakes are measured. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Most earthquakes happen along faults near plate boundaries. Friction locks the rocks on either side while the plates keep moving, so the rocks bend and store elastic energy. When the stress exceeds the strength of the fault, the rocks slip suddenly and spring back, releasing the energy as seismic waves. This is called elastic rebound. The point underground where slip begins is the focus, or hypocenter; the point on the surface directly above it is the epicenter.",
      "Body waves travel through Earth's interior. Primary (P) waves are compressional, pushing and pulling rock along their direction of travel; they are the fastest and pass through solids, liquids and gases. Secondary (S) waves are shear waves that move rock side to side; they are slower and cannot travel through liquids. Surface waves arrive last but often shake the ground hardest and cause most damage.",
      "Because P waves outrun S waves, the gap between their arrival times grows with distance from the focus. Each seismograph station's S-minus-P time gives its distance to the earthquake, and circles of those distances drawn from at least three stations meet at the epicenter. The same waves reveal Earth's interior: S waves do not pass through the outer core, which casts an S-wave shadow zone and shows that the outer core is liquid.",
      "Magnitude measures the energy an earthquake releases; today seismologists use the moment magnitude scale. It is logarithmic: each whole number up means about ten times greater ground motion on a seismogram and about 32 times more energy. Intensity, measured on scales such as the Modified Mercalli scale, describes the shaking and damage at a particular place, so one earthquake has one magnitude but many intensities."
    ],
