        "facts": ["Friction locks a fault while plates keep moving.", "Rock stores elastic energy as it bends.", "Sudden slip releases the energy as seismic waves.", "The focus is underground; the epicenter is on the surface above it."],
        "misconceptions": ["Earthquakes are caused by the ground cracking open into gaps.", "The epicenter is where the rock first slips."] },
      { "key": "waves", "title": "Compare P, S and surface waves", "description": "Contrasts the motion, speed and media of each wave type.",
        "concept": "Seismic wave types", "definition": "P waves are fast compressional waves through any medium; S waves are slower shear waves through solids only; surface waves come last and do most damage.",
        "facts": ["P waves are compressional and fastest.", "P waves travel through solids, liquids and gases.", "S waves are shear waves and cannot cross liquids.", "Surface waves arrive last and often cause most damage."],
        "misconceptions": ["S waves are faster than P waves.", "All seismic waves travel through the liquid outer core."] },
      { "key": "locating", "title": "Explain how epicenters and Earth's interior are found", "description": "Uses S-minus-P lag and three stations, and explains what the S-wave shadow zone shows.",
        "concept": "Locating earthquakes and probing the interior", "definition": "S-minus-P arrival times give distances from three stations whose circles meet at the epicenter; the S-wave shadow shows a liquid outer core.",
