    "title": "Geologic Time and Dating Rocks",
    "category": "earth-space",
    "subject": "Earth Science",
    "topic": "Geologic time",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you how geologists put rock layers in order and how they assign ages in years. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Earth is about 4.54 billion years old. Geologists divide that span into eons, eras, periods and epochs, marked largely by changes in the fossil record. Humans have existed for only a tiny fraction of it: modern humans for roughly 300,000 years.",
      "Relative dating puts events in order without giving ages in years. By the principle of superposition, in undisturbed layers the oldest rock is at the bottom and the youngest on top. Layers are originally laid down horizontally, so tilted or folded layers were disturbed later. A fault or an intrusion of magma that cuts across layers is younger than the layers it cuts. An unconformity is a gap in the record where rock was eroded away or never deposited.",
      "Fossils help match layers in different places. Index fossils come from organisms that were widespread but lived for a short time, so finding one dates a layer to that time span wherever it occurs.",
      "Absolute dating gives ages in years using radioactive decay. A radioactive parent isotope decays into a daughter isotope at a constant rate, measured by its half-life, the time for half the parent atoms to decay. After one half-life half the parent remains, after two a quarter, after three an eighth. Carbon-14, with a half-life of about 5,730 years, dates once-living material up to about 50,000 years old; uranium-lead dating, with half-lives of hundreds of millions to billions of years, dates the oldest rocks. Radiometric dates come mainly from igneous rocks, so sedimentary layers are often dated by the igneous rocks above and below them."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "relative-dating", "title": "Apply the principles of relative dating", "description": "Uses superposition, original horizontality and cross-cutting relationships to order events.",
        "concept": "Relative dating", "definition": "Relative dating orders rock layers and events without assigning numerical ages.",
        "facts": ["In undisturbed layers the oldest is at the bottom.", "Layers are laid down horizontally, so tilting came later.", "A feature that cuts across layers is younger than they are.", "An unconformity is a gap in the rock record."],
        "misconceptions": ["Relative dating tells how many years old a rock is.", "The top layer is always the youngest even after folding or overturning."] },
      { "key": "index-fossils", "title": "Explain how index fossils correlate layers", "description": "Explains why a good index fossil is widespread and short-lived.",
        "concept": "Index fossils", "definition": "Fossils of widespread, short-lived species date and match layers across regions.",
        "facts": ["Index fossil species were widespread.", "They existed for a short span of time.", "They let geologists match layers in distant places."],
        "misconceptions": ["Any fossil is equally useful for dating a layer."] },
      { "key": "half-life", "title": "Explain radiometric dating and half-life", "description": "Works out remaining parent after several half-lives and explains why decay is a reliable clock.",
        "concept": "Radiometric dating", "definition": "The constant decay of parent isotopes into daughter isotopes, measured by half-life, gives absolute ages.",
        "facts": ["A half-life is the time for half the parent atoms to decay.", "After two half-lives a quarter of the parent remains.", "Decay rates are constant and unaffected by ordinary heat or pressure."],
        "misconceptions": ["After two half-lives all of the parent isotope is gone.", "Each half-life is shorter than the last."] },
      { "key": "choosing-method", "title": "Choose a suitable dating method", "description": "Explains when carbon-14 or uranium-lead dating fits and how sedimentary layers are dated.",
        "concept": "Choosing a dating method", "definition": "The isotope must suit the material and age range; igneous rocks bracket sedimentary layers.",
        "facts": ["Carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years.", "Carbon-14 dates once-living material up to about 50,000 years.", "Uranium-lead dates the oldest rocks.", "Sedimentary layers are often dated by the igneous rocks above and below."],
        "misconceptions": ["Carbon-14 dating shows dinosaur bones are millions of years old.", "Earth is about 6,000 years old according to radiometric dating."] }
    ],
