  {
    "title": "The Water Cycle and Groundwater",
    "category": "earth-space",
    "subject": "Earth Science",
    "topic": "Hydrology",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you how water moves through the water cycle, where Earth's water is stored and how groundwater behaves. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "The water cycle is the continuous movement of water among the oceans, the air and the land. Energy from the Sun evaporates water from oceans, lakes and soil, and plants release water vapor through their leaves in transpiration. Rising air cools, and the vapor condenses on tiny particles into cloud droplets or ice crystals. When these grow heavy enough they fall as precipitation, and gravity carries the water back toward the sea.",
      "About 97 percent of Earth's water is salt water in the oceans. Of the small share that is fresh, roughly two-thirds is locked in glaciers and ice sheets and most of the rest is groundwater; rivers and lakes hold only a tiny fraction. Water can stay in one reservoir for very different times: days in the atmosphere, but thousands of years in deep ocean water, ice sheets or deep groundwater.",
      "Precipitation that reaches the ground either runs off over the surface into streams or soaks in by infiltration. How much soaks in depends on the soil, the slope, the vegetation and how much rain has already fallen; paved surfaces increase runoff and the risk of floods.",
      "Water that infiltrates fills the pore spaces in soil and rock. Below the water table every pore is saturated. Porosity is how much open space a material has; permeability is how easily water flows through it. An aquifer is a body of rock or sediment, such as sand, gravel or fractured limestone, that both stores and transmits enough water to supply wells. Clay can be very porous yet nearly impermeable. Groundwater mostly fills pores and cracks rather than forming underground lakes, and pumping it faster than it is recharged lowers the water table."
    ],
