        "facts": ["About 97 percent of Earth's water is in the oceans.", "Most fresh water is in ice sheets and glaciers.", "Groundwater holds far more fresh water than rivers and lakes.", "Water stays days in the air but can stay thousands of years in ice or deep groundwater."],
        "misconceptions": ["Most of Earth's fresh water is in lakes and rivers."] },
      { "key": "infiltration", "title": "Explain infiltration and runoff", "description": "Explains what decides whether rain soaks in or runs off, including human effects.",
        "concept": "Infiltration and runoff", "definition": "Rain soaks into the ground or flows over it, depending on soil, slope, vegetation, saturation and paving.",
        "facts": ["Infiltration is water soaking into the ground.", "Saturated or steep ground increases runoff.", "Pavement increases runoff and flood risk."],
        "misconceptions": ["All rain that falls on land soaks into the ground."] },
      { "key": "groundwater", "title": "Explain aquifers, porosity and permeability", "description": "Distinguishes porosity from permeability and explains what makes a good aquifer.",
        "concept": "Groundwater and aquifers", "definition": "Below the water table pores are saturated; an aquifer is permeable material that stores and transmits water.",
