    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you what drives surface and deep ocean currents, why upwelling matters and what causes the tides. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Surface currents, in roughly the top few hundred meters, are driven mainly by the prevailing winds. The Coriolis effect and the shapes of the continents turn them into large circular systems called gyres, which rotate clockwise in the Northern Hemisphere and counterclockwise in the Southern. Warm currents such as the Gulf Stream carry heat from the tropics toward the poles and give western Europe milder winters than places at the same latitude in eastern Canada.",
      "Deep currents are driven by differences in density, which depend on temperature and salinity; this is called thermohaline circulation. In the North Atlantic and around Antarctica, surface water is cooled and becomes saltier as sea ice forms and leaves salt behind. This cold, salty, dense water sinks and spreads slowly along the ocean floor, rising again elsewhere over centuries. Together, surface and deep currents form a global conveyor that moves heat around the planet.",
      "Upwelling happens where winds push surface water away from a coast or apart in the open ocean, and cold, nutrient-rich water rises from below to replace it. The nutrients feed plankton, so upwelling zones such as the coasts of Peru and California support some of the world's richest fisheries. During El Niño, upwelling off Peru weakens and the eastern Pacific warms.",
      "Tides are caused mainly by the Moon's gravity, which pulls more strongly on the side of Earth facing it than on Earth's center, and more strongly on the center than on the far side. This difference stretches the oceans into two bulges, one facing the Moon and one opposite it. As Earth rotates, most coasts pass through both bulges, giving about two high tides and two low tides every 24 hours and 50 minutes. The Sun's effect is a little under half the Moon's: when Sun, Moon and Earth line up at new and full moon their effects add to make spring tides with the largest range, and at the quarter moons they partly cancel to make neap tides."
    ],
