    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you what causes the seasons, why the Moon shows phases and when eclipses can happen. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Earth's axis is tilted about 23.5 degrees from perpendicular to the plane of its orbit, and it keeps pointing the same way in space all year. When the Northern Hemisphere is tilted toward the Sun, the Sun climbs higher in its sky, sunlight strikes the ground more directly and so is more concentrated, and days are longer: it is summer there and winter in the Southern Hemisphere. Six months later the situation is reversed.",
      "Distance from the Sun does not cause the seasons. Earth's orbit is nearly circular, and Earth is actually closest to the Sun in early January, during the northern winter. The hemispheres also have opposite seasons at the same time, which a change in distance could not explain.",
      "The Moon gives off no light of its own; half of it is always lit by the Sun. As the Moon orbits Earth, we see different portions of that sunlit half. At new moon the Moon lies roughly between Earth and Sun and its lit side faces away from us; at full moon it is on the far side of Earth and we see the whole lit face. The cycle of phases takes about 29.5 days. Earth's shadow does not cause the phases. The Moon also rotates once per orbit, so the same side always faces Earth.",
      "A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes directly between the Sun and Earth, which is possible only at new moon. A lunar eclipse happens when Earth's shadow falls on the Moon, possible only at full moon. Eclipses do not happen every month because the Moon's orbit is tilted about 5 degrees to Earth's, so at most new and full moons the Moon passes above or below the Sun-Earth line."
    ],
