  {
    "title": "The Life Cycles of Stars",
    "category": "earth-space",
    "subject": "Astronomy",
    "topic": "Stellar evolution",
    "instructions": "The examiner will ask you how stars form and shine, how astronomers classify them, and how a star's mass decides its fate. Answer in your own words and explain why, not only what. Say \"I don't know\" if you don't.",
    "passage": [
      "Stars form in cold, dense clouds of gas and dust called nebulae. Gravity pulls a clump of the cloud together; as it contracts it heats up, and when the core reaches about 10 million kelvin, hydrogen nuclei begin fusing into helium. The energy released makes the star shine, and the outward pressure of the hot gas balances the inward pull of gravity, a state called hydrostatic equilibrium.",
      "A star spends about 90 percent of its life on the main sequence, steadily fusing hydrogen in its core. The Hertzsprung-Russell diagram plots stars' luminosity against their surface temperature; most stars fall on a diagonal band, the main sequence, running from hot, bright, blue stars to cool, dim, red ones. A star's color shows its surface temperature: blue stars are hottest and red stars coolest. Along the main sequence, more massive stars are hotter and far more luminous.",
      "Mass decides how long a star lives. Massive stars burn their fuel so much faster that they live only a few million years, while the Sun, about 4.6 billion years old, has a main-sequence life of about 10 billion years, and the smallest red dwarfs can last trillions. When a Sun-like star runs out of hydrogen in its core, it swells into a red giant, later sheds its outer layers as a planetary nebula, and leaves behind its hot, dense core as a white dwarf, which slowly cools.",
      "A star born with more than about eight times the Sun's mass fuses heavier and heavier elements up to iron. Fusing iron releases no energy, so the core collapses in a fraction of a second and the star explodes as a supernova. The core is left as a neutron star or, for the most massive stars, a black hole. Elements up to iron are made by fusion in stars, and many heavier elements are made in supernovae and in mergers of neutron stars, so the carbon, oxygen and iron in our bodies were made in earlier generations of stars."
    ],
    "objectives": [
      { "key": "formation", "title": "Explain how stars form and why they shine", "description": "Traces collapse of a nebula to fusion and explains the balance that keeps a star stable.",
        "concept": "Star formation and fusion", "definition": "Gravity collapses a gas cloud until its core is hot enough for hydrogen fusion; pressure from that energy balances gravity.",
        "facts": ["Stars form in nebulae of gas and dust.", "Contraction heats the core.", "Stars shine by fusing hydrogen into helium.", "Gas pressure balances gravity in a stable star."],
        "misconceptions": ["Stars shine because they are burning like a fire.", "Stars are held together by their own pressure, with no role for gravity."] },
      { "key": "hr-diagram", "title": "Interpret the Hertzsprung-Russell diagram", "description": "Explains the axes, the main sequence and what a star's color tells us.",
        "concept": "The Hertzsprung-Russell diagram", "definition": "The H-R diagram plots luminosity against temperature; most stars lie on the main sequence.",
